Z||000013||theogre||04-13-99||08:47 AM||||you think driving a torch gets annoying at times, try driving a bomb. Ford Pinto never got rid of that image even tho it applied to to pre73-74 modles. Pinto production ended with the 80 modle year. Even today, people ask "Isn't that the car that explodes if you kick the back bumper?"<p>What was really annoying was that Ford knew about that problem before the first car ever sold. The Iacoca lead Ford administraion over ruled adding a 2$ shield to the tank.
Z||000014||theogre||04-13-99||09:03 AM||||after rereading the thread...... I try to keep fire supression in all my cars. Not just the toarches and bombs. You never know when some dumb shit will get inhaled into the engine compartment, or a fuel line pops, which is a very real threat on All fuel pump in tank cars.<p>I've found with allot of cars, you can put one of those little kitchen sized extiguishers next to the drivers seat. You could also put a 5-10BC rated unit in the front compartment of Fiero. Carefullly planed, you could put quite a bit of safety gear up there.
Z||000015||ChadMan||04-16-99||07:13 AM||||I've heard that "you're gonna die in a fire" talk from a lot of people, but I think my chances of being run over by a poorly-driven SUV are much higher!  Fieros have served as my primary means of transportation since 4/92 (84 coupe, 86 SE v6, 88 coupe, 91K total miles driven) and I've never had any problems with overheating or fires.  But I always pack an extinguisher just in case!
Z||000016||Cliff Pennock||04-16-99||11:39 AM||||Yes, I also have a fire extinguisher in my Fiero. Although I'm not sure if the engine-compartment is the best place to store it...  ;)<br>
Z||000017||theogre||04-16-99||01:20 PM||||cliff, you silly bugger..... ((Ogre shakes his head at the silly boy.))<p>That is about the worst place to put it. If you lift the lid to get to the extiguisher you could get hit in the face by the fire. You could always hope it explodes and blows the fire out......<p>I'd sugest putting it up with the spare tire. I like CO2, or if you can find it Halon. You can just spray those into the vents and up from undernieth to knock down the majority of it before you lift the deck lid.
Z||000018||Cliff Pennock||04-16-99||01:35 PM||||:) I know Ogre, but I still haven't come around installing the thing in the front compartment...
Z||000019||Motorhead||04-16-99||03:47 PM||||You guys have got me rolling on the floor laughing!!!<br>Good call Ogre, great rebound Cliff!<br>Halon extinguishers are available at most marine supply stores. Try Boaters World or West Marine. If you really want to get exotic, they've even got automatic detection and extingishing systems utilizing halon.
Z||000020||theogre||04-18-99||03:28 PM||||Actually I figured Cliff knew better, but allot of people don't. I don't even worry about "mounting" them. I just wire tie them to the spare so they don't bounce all over. The one next to the seat just sits there very nicely..... unless I hit the brakes REALLY hard.<p>U.S. companies that still sell halon? must be a big hold over of 1211/1301 old stock. They can't/won't make either of those any more.<p>I don't trust auto deploy too much. I've seen to many that either false trip, or the whole place is melting. (Ansul systems in resteraunts are great for these problems.) Now a driver controlled remote trip, this is a good thing. (falsing is allot of why Ansul systems Always have manual trips as well.)
